\citation{eROSITA_DR1}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces  LAMOST DR10 training-bias artifact. \textit  {Left:} Distribution of the peak-residual wavelength for LAMOST anomalies (green) vs.\ the expected uniform distribution (gray). The extreme concentration below 4500~\r A\ (LAMOST B arm) — containing 98\% of all anomalies — is a hallmark of training-set bias: objects observed at higher airmass or with sub-optimal blue-arm calibration are flagged as anomalous relative to a training set that does not adequately represent those conditions. \textit  {Right:} Composition breakdown of the 44{,}075 LAMOST anomalies. Only $\sim $2\% pass the consistency checks expected of genuine astrophysical anomalies. This is the clearest demonstration in the paper of how an imperfect training set produces a contaminated anomaly catalog.}}{15}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:lamost}{{7}{15}{LAMOST DR10 training-bias artifact. \textit {Left:} Distribution of the peak-residual wavelength for LAMOST anomalies (green) vs.\ the expected uniform distribution (gray). The extreme concentration below 4500~\AA \ (LAMOST B arm) — containing 98\% of all anomalies — is a hallmark of training-set bias: objects observed at higher airmass or with sub-optimal blue-arm calibration are flagged as anomalous relative to a training set that does not adequately represent those conditions. \textit {Right:} Composition breakdown of the 44{,}075 LAMOST anomalies. Only $\sim $2\% pass the consistency checks expected of genuine astrophysical anomalies. This is the clearest demonstration in the paper of how an imperfect training set produces a contaminated anomaly catalog}{figure.7}{}}
